  #standard-article(
  title: [Weekly Review],
  author: [Harper’s Magazine],
  source-name: [Harper's Magazine],
  images: (),
  paragraphs: (
  [More than 1,000 cargo ships were impeded from passing through the Strait of Hormuz, a maritime passage between Iran and Oman through which nearly a quarter of the world’s oil shipment travels, after Iran began attacking ships and placing mines in the strait. 1 2 3 The Iranian government offered use of the strait to any Arab or European country willing to expel its U. S. and Israeli envoys, and reportedly considered permitting certain ships’ passage with the provision that the oil be traded in Chinese yuan. 4 5 The U. S. military attacked Iran’s Kharg Island, where around 90 percent of the country’s oil exports are processed. 6 “We may hit it a few more times,” said the U. S. president, “just for fun.” 7 Israel struck multiple targets across Lebanon, including an apartment building in central Beirut, killing at least 64 people; and the Dubai International Airport temporarily suspended all of its flights after a drone strike. 8 9 The U. S. secretary of war, who reportedly spent nearly \$7 million dollars on lobster tail last year, claimed that Iran’s new supreme leader is “likely disfigured” and called for “no quarter” for the country’s combatants, thereby instructing that captured enemy soldiers not be kept as prisoners but executed. 10 11 12 Crude-oil prices surged to more than \$100 per barrel; the Pentagon informed Congress that the first week of its war with Iran cost \$11.3 billion; and multiple U. S. intelligence reports found that Iran’s government is not in danger of collapse. 13 14 15 16 The Federal Bureau of Investigation warned California police departments that Iran may be planning to use drones to launch an attack on the West Coast, in an advisory later revealed to be based on unverified intelligence. 17 “Iran does not want to harm ordinary Americans,” Iran’s foreign minister posted online, “who overwhelmingly voted to end involvement in costly foreign wars.” 18 The commander of the U. S. Central Command explained how AI was being deployed in warfare to assist in “seconds” with decision-making that used to take days, and the chair of the Federal Communications Commission threatened to revoke networks’ broadcasting licenses over coverage of the war. 19 20 Neither the U. S. secretary of war nor the White House press secretary ruled out the possibility of implementing a draft, a federal measure not utilized since the Vietnam War ; and t housands of U. S. troops were ordered to the Middle East in a Marine Expeditionary Unit, a development that has drawn parallels to the Vietnam War, wherein the first U. S. combat troops to make landfall were likewise from a Marine Expeditionary Unit. The hotline for U. S. service members and conscientious objectors received hundreds of calls. 21 22 23 24 25],
  [In Norfolk, Virginia, a former member of the National Guard who was released three years early from an 11-year prison sentence after attempting to send \$500 to the Islamic State and who once professed his desire to “murder U. S. military personnel” in a Fort Hood–style attack opened fire on a classroom at Old Dominion University, killing one and wounding two. 26 27 28 The students in the classroom “rendered him no longer alive,” said a special agent from the FBI’s Norfolk field office.“I don’t know how else to say it.” 29 In the state’s capitol, gun-rights activists distributed free 30-round magazines. 30 In Michigan, a man drove a pickup truck into a synagogue where more than a hundred children were congregating. 31 In California, a driver crashed into a highway-patrol car because he was watching YouTube videos and did not see the vehicle ahead of him. 32 In Texas, eight of nine protestors facing conviction for participating in the nonfatal shooting of a police officer outside an Immigration and Customs Enforcement detention center received charges of federal terrorism after being labeled “Antifa,” in a trial that cited their zines, book club, all-black attire, and usage of the messaging app Signal as proof of their domestic terrorism. 33 34 “Today’s verdict on terrorism charges will not be the last as the Trump Administration systematically dismantles Antifa,” said the U. S. attorney general. 35 A 51-year-old inmate became the sixth man to be executed on death row this year, using his last words to say that he hopes he can meet his victims in heaven and “tell them I’m sorry face to face.” 36 37 A woman collapsed at a Trump rally in Kentucky, prompting the president to request “Ave Maria” to be played as Doctor Oz tended to the woman. 38 “It will take me one year to recover. And that is an understatement,” said the singer Morissey after canceling his concert in Spain owing to sleep deprivation caused by the noise from a traditional Spanish festival taking place near his hotel. 39],
  [A Florida priest received more than \$500,000 in fines for feeding the homeless; a man in Toronto stole 91 tubs of ghee; hundreds of pounds of unrequested French-onion dip arrived at restaurants across Philadelphia; and a woman in Iowa was charged with an aggravated misdemeanor, among other charges, after delivering a family-size pan of lasagna laced with drugs to a pregnant woman in an attempt to induce a miscarriage. 40 41 42 43 A man in Chicago announced his plans to introduce 29 new varieties of tomatoes to the city by the end of the year. “Those tasteless travesties in supermarkets are not tomatoes,” said the man. “They’re red water balloons, and people should taste a real tomato.” 44 Millions of people with Italian heritage lost their rights to citizenship overnight. 45 A topless woman in Cleveland pretended to be unconscious to teach her daughter a lesson about drinking, and human waste flowed into the Willamette River in Portland, Oregon. 46 47 More than 500 alpacas gathered in Fort Wayne, Indiana; and a red fox stowed away on a cargo ship voyaging from Southampton, England, to New York City. “Clearly it fancied swapping the Solent for the Staten Island Ferry,” said a spokesman for Associated British Ports. 48 49 “Though next time, we’d recommend it consider the Queen Mary 2, which offers the Southampton-to-New York route with considerably more comfort!” 50 —Chloe Arnold],
  [The post Weekly Review appeared first on Harper's Magazine .],
),
